# Security & Secrets

- **Never commit secrets or `.env` files.** `.gitignore` already excludes `.env*`; don't add credentials to code, fixtures, or commit messages. Document env var names in settings schema / `.env.example` when needed, not values.
- **AI provider API keys** (OpenAI, Anthropic, Google, OpenRouter, etc.) belong in VS Code secure storage / user settings — never hardcode in `src/vs/ai/` or ship them in the renderer bundle. Don't log keys or echo them in error messages.
- **Don't send workspace file contents to providers without user intent.** Respect privacy settings and local-only mode; filter obvious secrets (tokens, private keys) before outbound AI requests where the product already does so.
- **Prompt injection is separate from secret redaction.** User- or third-party-controlled text (open files, terminal output, pasted diffs) must stay in clearly delimited data sections of prompts under `src/vs/ai/` — never concatenate untrusted content into the instruction portion. See `50-security` patterns in sibling monorepos for the general idea; adapt to the IDE provider pipeline.
- **Upstream VS Code security boundaries still apply.** Minimize core `src/vs/*` edits; keep AI code under `src/vs/ai/` (see `00-vscode-fork-conventions.mdc`).
